WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.560
Anomalous creatures in the SCP Foundation’s
universe are not a rarity.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.920
Dealing with anomalous sapient lifeforms is
the Foundation’s bread and butter - whether

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.160
it’s a talkative sea slug or a body-stealing
swarm of ants.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.910
For those of you who haven’t read their
Ethics Committee guidebooks, sapient anomalies

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:23.070
are defined as creatures who have a keen sense
of self-awareness and can communicate.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.821
From the zaniness of Doctor Bright and his
amulet to the neck-cracking SCP-173, these

00:00:28.821 --> 00:00:32.860
sapient anomalous entities come in all shapes
and sizes.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:38.410
But when does a creature become so sapient
they can be considered anomalous?

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:43.230
Take the humble octopus for example, who also
just so happens to be the subject of today’s

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:48.690
video, the octopus is a boneless, eight-legged
creature capable of shifting the color and

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.770
texture of its skin, and will blast predators
with a black substance to blind them.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:58.720
If I read that in an SCP Foundation file,
I might think those researchers found an alien

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:05.109
creature, but no, octopi – or octopodes,
are, as you know, real living breathing creatures

00:01:05.109 --> 00:01:07.710
you can see at any aquarium.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.399
Another interesting quirk of octopodes is
their intelligence.

00:01:11.399 --> 00:01:16.310
These creatures are smart, extremely smart,
hell, they will even use fallen coconuts as

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.580
a sort of armor against enemies.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.229
If tool use isn’t intelligence, I don’t
know what is.

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:26.689
You may be wondering, how can you make an
octopus more anomalous than what I’ve already

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:27.689
described.

00:01:27.689 --> 00:01:34.119
Well, today on SCP Explained, we will be taking
a look at SCP-2967 – The Sapient Cephalopods

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:36.530
- to answer that exact question.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:41.940
We’ve all probably been to aquariums, dark,
ambient rooms where the only light comes from

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.509
behind the massive tanks filled to the brim
with colorful fish and crustaceans.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:50.701
They can be fun to explore, but when you’ve
seen a bunch of exotic fish once, it’s not

00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:53.640
like you’re clamoring to get back in soon.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.749
This was the problem faced by the Galaxy Aquarium
in May of 2010 – attendance was low, and

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:01.119
profit was lower.

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:04.619
The owner of the Aquarium was a man by the
name of Mark Hemphill.

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:07.689
He was young and extremely ambitious.

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:11.790
Mark had an interest in marine life since
when he was a child and dedicated his life

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.150
to studying the creatures of the sea.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.510
When he opened the aquarium, he had just gotten
out of college.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:22.440
He hoped his passion and his aquarium would
inspire even just one child to pursue the

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:24.599
study of marine life as he did.

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:29.560
Mark’s ambitious personality was not going
to let him watch his life’s work go to waste.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.260
At a nearby auction house, the anomalous Group
of Interest Marshall, Carter, and Dark were

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:39.120
organizing a showing for their new batch of
anomalous creatures available for bidding.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.430
Seasoned viewers of SCP Explained have likely
run into anomalies that Marshall, Carter,

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:45.330
and Dark have had a hand in.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:50.629
For those not in the loop, Marshal, Carter,
and Dark are dealers of anomalous goods and

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:55.090
services, selling anomalies to the highest
bidder no matter who they are, from the average

00:02:55.090 --> 00:03:01.670
Joe to a tyrannical dictator, Marshall, Carter,
and Dark only care about cold hard cash and

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:04.090
getting as much of it as they can.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.879
Mark, hearing whispers of a massive auction
coming up, decided to attend.

00:03:07.879 --> 00:03:13.629
He entered the auction house, an old, dilapidated
warehouse on the sketchy side of town.

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:19.489
Not exactly inspiring confidence, but Mark
thought maybe, just maybe, there would be

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:23.209
something for him to add to his aquarium to
get more attendees.

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:26.859
Mark walked through the heavy metal doors
into an immaculate auction space.

00:03:26.859 --> 00:03:30.569
The building was loaded with well-dressed
individuals who gazed over the items they

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:32.180
had for sale.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:37.360
Wooden tables lined the walls of the warehouse,
and on top of each table were tanks and cages

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.359
of creatures Mark never thought could exist,
from dogs that smelled like candles to purple

00:03:42.359 --> 00:03:46.450
frogs that telepathically communicated with
the people around them.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:50.390
Mark was convinced that there was something
here he could use.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:55.209
A staff member approached Mark almost immediately;
it was as if the staff could smell the willingness

00:03:55.209 --> 00:03:56.859
to spend money on him.

00:03:56.859 --> 00:04:00.609
She was an older woman, dressed in a fine
purple-hued suit.

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:05.270
She spoke with a comforting candor to Mark,
asking him about his day and what brought

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.030
him to the auction house.

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:11.569
She introduced herself as “J” and said
that the auction house had just the thing

00:04:11.569 --> 00:04:14.260
for Mark before he could even get the words
out.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.040
They walked to the opposite side of the building,
where a purple curtain was erected.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.960
Walking through, it was as if Mark has returned
back to his aquarium.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.440
What were the odds that this place would have
a marine life section?

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.850
Oddly, for such a well-designed room, there
was only one small tank placed in it.

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:36.139
Within it was six Octopodes, a pale yellow
in color – Mark immediately recognized the

00:04:36.139 --> 00:04:38.560
species as the Common Octopus.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:43.100
The Galaxy Aquarium already had a cephalopod
section with much more rare species than the

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.300
Common Octopus.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:49.030
Dejected, Mark almost gave up hope before
J approached the tank and kneeled.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:53.030
J seemed concerned, then called out the name
Levi.

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:58.650
From under the table on which the tank stood,
a massive octopus slithered into sight, startling

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.650
Mark.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.349
It was at least 5 feet in height and length.

00:05:02.349 --> 00:05:06.590
That’s impossible, Mark thought, there’s
no way that an octopus of this species could

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.460
get to be this large.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:11.610
The octopus’ size was not the only oddity
it held, though.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.530
J began to scold Levi for leaving its tank
and asked it what it had to say for itself.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:21.319
She threw a sharpie marker to the ground for
Levi, who uncapped it and began to write out

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:24.500
the word “SORRY” on the concrete floor.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.590
This couldn’t be, Mark thought, there’s
absolutely no way that an octopus can learn

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.210
to understand English, let alone write it.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:35.650
J turned to Mark, encouraging him to talk
to Levi.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.020
“What is your name?”

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:39.349
Mark asked sheepishly.

00:05:39.349 --> 00:05:42.069
He couldn’t believe he was trying to talk
to an octopus.

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:49.199
As soon as the words left his mouth, the octopus
spelled out L-E-V-I with its marker.

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:50.789
Mark couldn’t believe it.

00:05:50.789 --> 00:05:52.840
This had to be some trick, right?

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:56.780
Some sort of marketing gimmick to get him
to buy an overgrown piece of calamari.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:00.310
There is no way that an octopus could have
self-awareness.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.930
Mark knew they were smart, of course, he had
studied these things for years, but knowing

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:07.690
its name… that’s not possible.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.560
“What are you?”

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:13.509
Mark questioned, hoping to figure out if it
was an animatronic instead of a living breathing

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:14.509
animal.

00:06:14.509 --> 00:06:15.970
Levi began to write on the floor again.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:18.949
Not a word this time, but a symbol.

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:24.569
The octopus fist drew a large oval, then eight
lines coming from the bottom – a crude drawing

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:26.110
of an octopus.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:30.150
Behind Levi, the other six octopodes were
watching him as spectators, seeming to clap

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.009
when Levi finished the drawing.

00:06:32.009 --> 00:06:35.979
J, with a smug grin on her face, asked Mark
if he was impressed.

00:06:35.979 --> 00:06:39.740
The look on Mark’s face already told the
story, so J began to go over the pricing of

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:40.979
the creatures.

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:45.360
Levi and the other six octopodes were a package
deal, with the starting bid for all seven

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.240
placed at 3000 dollars.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.780
Mark, without a second thought, asked if he
could outright purchase Levi and his compatriots

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.370
for ten thousand dollars.

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:57.930
J’s smile grew wider, and she shook Mark’s
hand without another word said.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:02.360
Levi and the six other octopodes were delivered
to the Galaxy Aquarium the next day.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:07.009
Within that time, Mark had already prepared
an unused section for the arrival of his new

00:07:07.009 --> 00:07:08.009
attraction.

00:07:08.009 --> 00:07:12.300
The cephalopods were placed in their tanks
without much difficulty and settled into their

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:13.500
new home.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:18.970
Advertising began for the Galaxy Aquarium’s
newest attraction “The Talking Octopus”

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:22.069
– Mark even splurged on a billboard on the
nearby highway.

00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:28.030
This investment was meant to save the aquarium,
and for the next few months, attendance skyrocketed.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:32.250
Now we know why the SCP Foundation keeps a
heavy watch over anomalous entities in the

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:37.150
public because all it takes is one ambitious
person before the entire world knows that

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:38.530
anomalies exist.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:43.960
With the popularity of Levi, it was a matter
of time before the SCP Foundation heard of

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:44.960
it.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.520
It was time for an investigation.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:51.419
Site-12 was a site dedicated to Marine Life,
and as such, many of the personnel stationed

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:55.300
there had the appropriate expertise to deal
with the anomaly.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:59.500
Agent Hooper volunteered for the investigation;
he was ordered to pose as an animal behavior

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:04.500
specialist from the Sea Coral Pacific Federation
- Real creative there, SCP Foundation.

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:09.020
It just so happened that at the same time
Agent Hooper was getting involved, Levi was

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:12.259
beginning to give Mark a hard time at his
aquarium.

00:08:12.259 --> 00:08:15.150
It was a late night in January 2011.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:19.229
The aquarium had since closed after another
day of almost full attendance.

00:08:19.229 --> 00:08:24.600
Levi was not sleeping, however, he was hungry
and by coincidence, the section of the Galaxy

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:29.599
Aquarium next to Levi’s tank was a potential
all you could eat buffet should his plan come

00:08:29.599 --> 00:08:30.759
to fruition.

00:08:30.759 --> 00:08:34.880
The next day, aquarium staff awoke to the
filtration system of the crab enclosure being

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.669
blocked by one of Levi’s friends.

00:08:37.669 --> 00:08:40.570
It was quickly removed and placed back into
its proper tank.

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:45.630
It wasn’t out of the ordinary for Octopodes
to escape their tanks; as I explained, they’re

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:47.140
smart creatures.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:50.980
Aquarium staff began the process of moving
the crabs to a temporary tank while repairs

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.950
and cleaning on the original tank were taking
place.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:58.950
As their attention was drawn to the damaged
filter and contaminated crab tank, Levi managed

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:02.940
to escape and enter the unguarded temporary
tank of the crabs.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:07.890
Broken shells and water splattered the floor
of the temporary tank room as the feast began.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.070
It was a massacre.

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:15.070
Levi ate 20 of the 40 crabs and another 10
were dropped off into the octopus tank for

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:16.630
Levi’s comrades.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:20.640
Satisfied with its meal, Levi returned to
his tank as if nothing had happened.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.851
An hour passed before the leftovers of Levi’s
dinner were discovered, and it didn’t take

00:09:24.851 --> 00:09:29.370
much guessing on the part of Aquarium staff
to deduce who was behind this.

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.829
Agent Hooper arrived the next day and asked
to meet with Mark.

00:09:32.829 --> 00:09:37.490
Hooper explained that he was from the Sea
Coral Pacific Federation and his place of

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:40.960
employment had heard of Levi and wanted to
study them.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:44.950
Mark, like a moth to a flame, eagerly took
the opportunity to describe the remarkable

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:46.930
events of the previous day.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:52.080
Hooper nodded along with the story and promised
to figure out why Levi was acting this way.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:56.519
Agent Hooper was escorted to the Talking Octopus
section of the aquarium and asked for some

00:09:56.519 --> 00:09:58.870
privacy, which he was granted.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.120
Levi was sitting on a large mound of sand
while the smaller octopodes sat in front of

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:05.670
it as if they were intently listening.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:10.820
Levi was apparently lecturing the group, using
a sharp rock to carve words and pictures on

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:13.260
the plexiglass walls of the tank.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.370
Agent Hooper knocked on the glass, startling
the group.

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:20.240
In anger, Levi swam to the front of the tank
and stared at Agent Hooper.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.930
With a tentacle, it knocked hard on the glass
and then endeavored to return to its lecture.

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:29.640
Agent Hooper didn’t catch the hint that
he was unwelcome, and stayed uncomfortably

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:34.019
close to the glass until Levi couldn’t ignore
the agent any longer.

00:10:34.019 --> 00:10:39.510
The octopus held up five tentacles and made
a shooing motion towards Hooper, who was incredibly

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:40.670
surprised.

00:10:40.670 --> 00:10:44.779
Hooper recognized that motion as the phrase
“go away” in sign language.

00:10:44.779 --> 00:10:48.860
Hooper, in response, asked Levi “Sign Language?”

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.230
in sign language.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:53.670
In response, Levi once again held up five
tentacles and created what appeared to be

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:57.480
a fist, then bobbed it back and forth like
a head nodding.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.290
An octopus that knows sign language – not
the most dangerous or extraordinary anomaly

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:08.060
the Foundation has ever discovered, but still,
this anomaly could not remain out of containment.

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:12.930
Hooper quickly contacted the research head
at Site-12 and explained the situation; he

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:18.620
needed a containment van for an aquatic anomaly,
a containment force, and class A amnestics

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:19.839
immediately.

00:11:19.839 --> 00:11:21.480
This request was granted.

00:11:21.480 --> 00:11:26.149
Foundation Mobile Task Force Lambda-12, "Pest
Control", stormed the building and wrangled

00:11:26.149 --> 00:11:29.790
the aquarium staff into a closet so they couldn’t
interfere.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:33.750
The agents were dressed in their tactical
armor and held large rifles, but this was

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:38.600
only to show that they were meant to be taken
seriously, and not to be used for force.

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:42.350
A white, unmarked containment van arrived
soon after.

00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:46.640
Foundation researchers ran into the building
and transported Levi and his students into

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:51.520
the temporary tanks of the containment van,
and made sure they were securely closed and

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:52.899
locked multiple times.

00:11:52.899 --> 00:11:57.459
When each octopus was secured, an airborne
version of A-Class Amnestics was distributed

00:11:57.459 --> 00:12:01.420
throughout the aquarium by the Mobile Task
Force agents leading to all knowledge of Levi

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:05.560
being forgotten by the aquarium staff and
Mark.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:10.170
For further containment, the SCP Foundation
distributed liquid amnestics into the city’s

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:14.829
water supply, so that anyone who saw one of
Levi’s performances would forget that it

00:12:14.829 --> 00:12:16.130
ever happened.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:23.310
The SCP Foundation classified Levi as SCP-2967
and discovered the full extent of its anomalous

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:24.640
attributes.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.180
SCP-2967 of course has the ability to communicate
through written language, is able to recognize

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:36.040
and identify colors, symbols, and individual
features of other species, and holds an abnormally

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:40.140
high intellect, almost comparable to an average
human’s intelligence.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:48.070
The six comrades of SCP-2967 were designated
as SCP-2967-1, as they do not seem to be at

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:52.290
the same level of intellect as SCP-2967.

00:12:52.290 --> 00:13:00.070
Through behavioral analysis and research,
it was found that SCP-2967 will refer to SCP-2967-1

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.930
as its students, friends, or army.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:10.610
Dr. Reynard, the head researcher on the SCP-2967
anomaly, began hosting “intelligence checkups”

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:14.350
on SCP-2967 every two weeks.

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:19.320
These consisted of Levi talking to Dr. Reynard
through the use of a waterproof magnetic slate

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:21.690
while the doctor asked Levi questions.

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:27.899
On one such occasion, the Foundation discovered
that there may be more creatures similar to

00:13:27.899 --> 00:13:29.830
SCP-2967 and its students.

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:36.010
Doctor Reynard began by drawing a crab, an
octopus, and SCP-2967’s toy.

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:39.190
He labeled them CRAB and FRIEND and TOY.

00:13:39.190 --> 00:13:44.610
On its slate, SCP-2967 wrote “like” and
then a sketch of a heart.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:51.570
Dr. Reynard drew a checkmark on his clipboard,
before rewarding SCP-2967 with a prawn.

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:55.800
Following up, Dr. Reynard asked if there was
anything Levi didn’t like.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:14:00.320
To display this concept, Dr. Reynard drew
an X over a heart symbol.

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:04.910
Levi paused for a moment, seeming to think,
then drew what appeared to be a bird with

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:07.520
outstretched wings and an angular beak.

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:12.130
Upon finishing the drawing, the octopus’
skin changed to a deep red color

00:14:12.130 --> 00:14:17.959
Levi wrote “Dont know word” on his slate,
then returned to its normal colorization.

00:14:17.959 --> 00:14:23.389
Reynard displayed a photo of a pigeon to Levi,
who marked an X on his slate, signifying that

00:14:23.389 --> 00:14:27.019
there was something wrong before shifting
its skin black.

00:14:27.019 --> 00:14:31.990
In response, Dr. Reynard returned a picture
of a crow to Levi, who scrambled to write

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:33.180
on its slate.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:35.509
“That ! Word ?” it wrote hastily.

00:14:35.509 --> 00:14:37.840
Reynard responded “CROW.”

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:42.290
With his newfound knowledge, Levi drew a heart
with an X through it on its slate, then spelled

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:47.019
out “DONT LIKE CROW !” Doctor Reynard
was confused.

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:51.269
How do two species of creatures who live in
completely different habitats have a disdain

00:14:51.269 --> 00:14:52.990
for each other?

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.760
SCP-2967 began to explain.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:00.630
Levi sketched an octopus, a crow, and what
appeared to be a human.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:05.880
Over the head of the human, Levi drew an arrow
pointing at the human, before drawing a checkmark

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:08.339
over the sketch of the octopus and the crow.

00:15:08.339 --> 00:15:12.420
Levi connected the checkmarks to the arrow
above the human with two lines.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:17.110
It seemed that the crows and the octopodes
disdain come from their common ability to

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.959
connect and grow fond of humans.

00:15:19.959 --> 00:15:24.449
The intelligence check concluded after Levi
explained this to Doctor Reynard, as the octopus

00:15:24.449 --> 00:15:28.230
claimed it was tired and wanted to return
to its containment chamber.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:33.470
The next day, however, it seemed that SCP-2967
had correctly predicted what would happen

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.029
at Site-12

00:15:35.029 --> 00:15:40.300
On April 3rd, 2011, research staff at Site
12 reported a high concentration of American

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.160
Crows surrounding the building.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:46.440
Many were perched in trees or on the roof
of the site, but some were simply hovering

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.930
near the entrances, seeming to hope that the
door would open.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:54.280
While the crows weren’t aggressive to humans,
they would fly to a window connected to the

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:58.620
containment chamber of SCP-2967 and peck on
it.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:04.209
This continued for hours, and it seems that
the crows were threatening Levi and its friends.

00:16:04.209 --> 00:16:08.649
The Foundation deployed Mobile Task Force
Lambda-4 "Birdwatchers” to Site 12.

00:16:08.649 --> 00:16:13.519
This team was not armed in your standard tactical
gear and heavy weaponry, they wore camouflage

00:16:13.519 --> 00:16:17.620
clothing, with shoes that muffled their footsteps
in grassy environments.

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:22.190
They had jackets of thick leather to prevent
injuries from talons or beaks, and they had

00:16:22.190 --> 00:16:26.650
specialized pellet guns they used to incapacitate
anomalous birds.

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:31.790
They began by shooing the birds away and captured
a few using nets for further study.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:35.500
As they concluded, all but one of the crows
dispersed.

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:41.279
This crow was, similar to Levi, much larger
compared to nonanomalous members of its species.

00:16:41.279 --> 00:16:46.019
The large crow flew ten feet from the entrance
of the site, and spat the decaying remains

00:16:46.019 --> 00:16:51.180
of one Common Octopus out onto the asphalt
parking lot, before retreating.

00:16:51.180 --> 00:16:55.790
The Birdwatchers attempted to follow the group
of crows but the flock was impossible to track.

00:16:55.790 --> 00:17:00.959
What was found, however, was an area in a
nearby forest that contained an abnormally

00:17:00.959 --> 00:17:06.520
large number of feathers belonging to the
American Crow species, as well as numerous

00:17:06.520 --> 00:17:11.510
small, makeshift huts made of sticks and dying
leaves, and a collection of various litter

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:16.560
fashioned into basic tools, such as hammers
made of bolts and toothbrushes.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:21.020
The most surprising item retrieved during
this exploration was the torn remains of a

00:17:21.020 --> 00:17:27.520
copy of the documentation for SCP-2967, kept
in the largest hut.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:32.350
As is standard, the SCP Foundation enacted
surveillance procedures on this location,

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:35.710
placing hidden cameras and microphones, as
well as traps.

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:39.250
The large crow has been classified as SCP-2967-B.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:44.580
So, it seems that the animal kingdom is at
war for the favor of the human race.

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:48.760
While the Foundation only knows for sure that
octopodes and crows are at battle, there may

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:52.790
be many more species out there who look to
curry our favor.

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:55.720
Who do you think will be the victor should
battle commence?

00:17:55.720 --> 00:18:02.530
Now go check out “SCP-247 - A Harmless Kitten”
and “SCP-1111 - The White Dog” for more

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:05.540
anomalous animals that defy expectations!

